  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1204 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nThe aforementioned low will continue to track north and eastward \nacross the Intermountain West on Friday. Much of the day should \nremain dry, however by the late afternoon/evening time frame its \ninfluence should encompass much of the area with embedded leading \nshortwaves combined with an increasing Pacific moisture fetch \nbringing the potential for showers and thunderstorms near the TX/NM \nborder. A secondary cutoff low will develop over the Desert \nSouthwest on Saturday and track across the Four Corners region into \nSunday. Showers and thunderstorms will slowly shift eastward during \nthis time, although remain generally confined to areas on the \nCaprock. Locally heavy rainfall may be possible, particularly with \ntraining storms. The cutoff low will phase with the main synoptic \nflow pattern on Monday creating a broad, positively tilted trough \nwill result. As it shifts southeastward along with the associated \nsurface cold front, the plume of Pacific moisture will overspread \nthe entire forecast area. Forecast confidence decreases after that \npoint, however it looks to be a wet start to next week at least \nthrough Tuesday afternoon. Highs behind the front look to only reach \nthe low-to-mid 60s.",
